## v0.3.0 - Initial Operators
Data operators (consume/transform value) vs control operators (gate on status).
[ ] :r / :rr - write / append running value to file
[ ] :i - read a file into the pipeline mid-chain (read back what :r wrote)
[ ] :s / :ss - success/failure conditionals, bash && / || semantics:
    running status passes through skipped segments, chains left-associative
[-] :: dropped - statements-are-lines + backslash continuation covers it; token stays reserved
[-] /print dropped - pipeline tail renders implicitly, no literal-sink command needed
[ ] Get good parsing patterns and predictable behaviors early. This is a wish.

### v0.3.0 Decision Notes
- Example command (success path)
```ptt
/file styles.css :ai@claude "Only output the corrected CSS" :r styles.css :s /persist styles.css
```
#### Thoughts on syntax (see docs/slash-colon-spec.md for full grammar):
- /command must be on newlines always.
- Commands are always command -> input -> params
- Data operators only use the preceding output as input; control operators
  (:s/:ss) gate the next segment on status without altering the value
- A :s/:ss segment runs a /command fresh (like RHS of && / ||)
- No globbing or loops to start with - don't want variables or list iteration or arrays or all the BASH boggins just yet.
- That kind of scripting can be done via combination of actual shell and the -c flag.

